  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government through the Ministry of Gender, Labour and Social Development (MGLSD), in consultation with Social Partners, developed and approved in April 2021, a gender-sensitive National Action Plan on Elimination of Child Labour (NAP II 2020/21 &#x2013; 2024/25).  NAP II was launched by the President during the National commemoration of the International Labour Day, May 1st 2021.

The review process was highly participatory and consultative involving all stakeholders. MGLSD constituted a tripartite committee in October 2020 to revise the NAP, and ensure that it was in alignment with the third National Development Plan (NDP III) and incorporated relevant COVID-19 related issues The committee completed its work in April 2021.  Thereafter, it was taken through the Ministry Senior and Top Policy management for approval and clearance before the launch
 ILO was part of the technical committee established to review and revise the NAP.  It provided inputs specifically on relevant ILO Instruments on Child labour and other core ILO Conventions. It reminded members of the Committee that Uganda was an Alliance 8.7 Pathfinder Country, and that the revision and adoption would be taking place during the International Year for the Elimination of Child Labour.  It also reminded members of the importance of ensuring a link to SDG Target 8.7.  

Additionally, with support from the Government of the Netherlands, in February 2021, the ILO signed an implementation agreement with the Government Ministry of Gender, Labour and Social Development to implement  an action programme covering several areas including technical support for launch and dissemination of the NAP</narrative>
   </description>
